WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.116 --> 00:00:04.836
- I, as the Snowflake
Consultant on the movie Frozen,

00:00:04.836 --> 00:00:07.005
they wanted to get all
the snowflakes right

00:00:07.005 --> 00:00:09.298
and so they asked me,
you know, how they grow

00:00:09.298 --> 00:00:11.293
and I was very pleased
when the movie came out,

00:00:11.293 --> 00:00:12.647
they all looked like real snowflakes,

00:00:12.647 --> 00:00:14.843
none of them were eight-sided,
they were all six-sided,

00:00:14.843 --> 00:00:16.176
and so, good job.

00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:19.895
My name is Ken Libbrecht, and
I'm a Professor of Physics

00:00:19.895 --> 00:00:23.478
here at Caltech, and I
also grow snowflakes.

00:00:26.791 --> 00:00:28.621
Well, I got into physics very early,

00:00:28.621 --> 00:00:30.870
and I've been doing that most of my life.

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:32.571
Maybe about 20 years ago
I just got interested

00:00:32.571 --> 00:00:35.721
in how crystals grow and
that sort of led me to ice

00:00:35.721 --> 00:00:39.061
and to snowflakes, and we
still don't exactly understand

00:00:39.061 --> 00:00:41.368
why they look like they
do, so it's kind of fun

00:00:41.368 --> 00:00:43.201
to try to think about.

00:00:44.896 --> 00:00:47.387
To make a snowflake in the
lab, you start with water

00:00:47.387 --> 00:00:49.643
and water vapor and you've
gotta get everything cold

00:00:49.643 --> 00:00:51.814
and basically start a crystal growing,

00:00:51.814 --> 00:00:55.059
and I'll kind of blow
moist air down onto it,

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:57.444
and it just absorbs
water vapor from the air

00:00:57.444 --> 00:00:59.982
and starts to grow and I can
do this on a piece of glass

00:00:59.982 --> 00:01:02.194
and so just watch it grow.

00:01:02.194 --> 00:01:05.175
I'm the only one that makes
snowflakes like this in the lab.

00:01:05.175 --> 00:01:06.855
When you start to look really carefully

00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:10.109
at how a snowflake works, you
find you don't understand it

00:01:10.109 --> 00:01:12.729
and there's a lot going
on and you dig deeper,

00:01:12.729 --> 00:01:14.141
there's more and more stuff

00:01:14.141 --> 00:01:18.566
and it's really very
interesting to me in a way.

00:01:18.566 --> 00:01:21.391
I love to go out and, sort of, hunt for snowflakes

00:01:21.391 --> 00:01:23.373
because these beautiful works of art

00:01:23.373 --> 00:01:27.038
are just falling all over
and you pick a few of them up

00:01:27.038 --> 00:01:28.630
and photograph them and the rest of them

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.007
you just trample on.

00:01:30.007 --> 00:01:32.034
At the end of the day,
I'm trying to understand

00:01:32.034 --> 00:01:35.423
how atoms and molecules fit
together to form crystals

00:01:35.423 --> 00:01:37.522
and by studying snowflakes,

00:01:37.522 --> 00:01:39.932
slowly you figure things
out that are, that are useful.

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:42.187
One snowflake at a time.

00:01:42.187 --> 00:01:44.437
(chuckles)

